thinking:
All evidence gathered. Findings:

1. **Font tokens broken at runtime (bug, high)** — layout.tsx injects `--font-geist-sans` (line 8) but globals.css `--font-mono: var(--font-geist-mono)` is the only geist reference; `--font-sans` token resolves to runtime `--font-sans` which IS injected (Inter). Hmm, Inter variable is `--font-sans` ✓ used on html. So `--font-sans` works. What's broken: `--font-geist-sans` is loaded on body but never consumed — dead asset. And `--font-geist-mono` → used by font-mono ✓ (on body ✓). So main bug: unused Geist sans font (performance/maintainability), and fragile self-referencing `--font-sans: var(--font-sans)` which is legal CSS here but confusing; also if someone removes Inter from layout, silently falls back. I'll comment on lines 10-11 as maintainability + the unused font as performance (the unused font is layout.tsx issue — but rule says findings outside current file must not be commented. The self-reference in globals.css IS the current file. I can mention that these tokens depend on external injection and that `--font-geist-sans` loaded upstream is never consumed here — frame the globals.css side: token names create hidden coupling). Category: maintainability, medium.

Actually let me reconsider whether `--font-sans: var(--font-sans)` is safe: Tailwind's `font-sans` utility generates `font-family: var(--font-sans)`. In @theme inline, `--font-sans` theme token points to runtime var of same name. No cycle because theme namespace is `--font-*` not emitted as CSS vars (inline mode). If someone removed `inline`, cycle → invalid. Comment: fragile, document requirement.

2. **Dark mode chart palette hue mismatch (bug/maintainability, medium)** — lines 69-73 vs 105-109.

3. **Primary contrast (a11y, medium/high)** — :root primary oklch(0.67 0.16 58) + primary-foreground oklch(0.99 0.02 95) on white bg (0.145 fg). Also `.dark` primary oklch(0.77 0.16 70) over dark bg 0.145 with fg 0.28. WCAG contrast from L*: white L*≈100, oklch L=0.67 → L*≈66.7 → contrast=(1.05)/(0.145+0.05)? Let me do rough: contrast = (Y1+0.05)/(Y2+0.05); Y ≈ L*^2 relative... white Y=1; L*=66.7 → Y≈(66.7/100)^2.4? Standard: Y = ((L*+16)/116)^3 for L*>8: (82.7/116)^3 = (0.713)^3 = 0.362. Contrast = 1.05/0.412 = 2.55 → fails AA (4.5, even 3.0 for large text). Solid finding: near-white text on medium-lightness orange primary likely fails AA; verify or darken primary / use dark foreground. Also primary on white background as button bg: white text vs orange 2.5:1 — fail. dark: 0.77 → L*=77 → Y=((93)/116)^3=0.512; fg 0.28 → L*=28 → Y=(44/116)^3=0.0459; contrast=0.562/0.0959=5.86 ✓ dark OK-ish. Light mode is the failure. severity medium (a11y). Category: other? Use "bug"? The categories: bug, security, performance, maintainability, test, style, documentation, other. Accessibility → "other" or "bug". I'll use "bug" with severity medium... I'll pick category "other", severity medium. Hmm, contrast failures are commonly tagged as bug. I'll use "bug".

4. **destructive foreground pairing** — destructive has no `-foreground` token anywhere (not in @theme nor :root). shadcn default theme includes `--destructive-foreground`. If any component (button destructive variant) uses `text-destructive-foreground` / `bg-destructive text-white`? shadcn v4 button destructive variant uses `text-white`. Hmm, latest shadcn button: `bg-destructive text-white`. So maybe fine. But kbd/theme referencing? Search would cost a call. The pre-scan said "the foreground pairing of destructive needs verification per WCAG AA" — destructive oklch(0.58 0.22 27) medium red; if components pair white text: 0.58→L*=58→Y=(74/116)^3=0.261; white contrast=1.05/0.311=3.4 <4.5 fail for normal text. But no destructive-foreground token in this file — I can fold this into the primary contrast comment or a separate one noting the token set lacks `--destructive-foreground` (inconsistent with other paired tokens like card/card-foreground), risking components referencing `text-destructive-foreground` failing. Actually Tailwind v4: `text-destructive-foreground` would not resolve → class dropped silently. Whether components use it — uncertain. I'll include as low/medium maintainability comment on the destructive line: missing paired -foreground token unlike every other color family; either intentional (text-white hardcoded in components) or an omission.

