thinking:
Confirmed: `handoffInitiator` carries `origin` (the root) in handoff initiators, and handoff-delivery passes `handoffInitiator(work.fromBotId, work.initiator)` as the run's initiator. So the run assertion's initiator DOES carry origin, and `authoriseCoordinationRun`'s comparison ignores it entirely — a run whose signed initiator says root=deployment would pass validation against work context with root=person (and vice versa). The claim's `work.initiator` also carries origin (delegatedWork schema includes it). So the gap is real: `workInitiator.origin` vs `runInitiator.origin` never compared. Practical exploitability depends on who forges RunAssertions — the assertion is deployment-signed (per "as this deployment signed it"), so the forging party is the deployment's own runner (handoff-runner铸造时用 handoffInitiator,两者都源自同一 work,正常路径一致)。But the check's purpose is proving the signed claim matches the signed run; skipping origin weakens defense in depth — e.g., if work payload was tampered or a mismatch between claim and run signed at different times (replay of run assertion with same key fields but different root). Since delegatedWork parses `origin: rootInitiator.optional()`, the origin is part of the claim context and validated nowhere. I'll report it as a maintainability/security (medium) issue: 逐字段比对遗漏 origin.

