```

That gives compile-time exhaustiveness via the mapped type annotation (TS checks all required keys present; though extra spread... with a typed annotation, excess property from spread isn't error, but missing keys are). This is a solid suggestion — eliminates the nested ternary AND the cast. I'll combine focus #2 and #3 into one comment (same lines) — actually better as one comment covering both aspects, since a single suggestion fixes both. But the checklist wants each issue separately? They're intertwined at the same lines; one comment with a suggestion fixing both is cleaner. Actually let me make them two separate comments? The instruction says batch comments. Two comments on the same lines might be noisy. I'll do one comment addressing the nested ternary + the unsafe cast together with the fix.

2. Focus #4: `amount === "0"` — since `canonicalPriceAmount` strips trailing zeros ("0.00" → "0"; fraction becomes "" → whole "0"... wait: "0.00" → whole="0", match[2]="00" → fraction after replace = "" → amount = "0". Yes canonical). And snapshots are built through canonical (line 249, 288-294 assert canonical). So `amount === "0"` is reliable — NOT a bug. But the redundant `status === "known"` check: the preceding loop returns early if any billed dimension is unknown... but wait — the `free` check runs `every` over TRANSCRIPTION_BILLED_DIMENSIONS again with `status === "known" &&`. Since the loop above already returned false for unknown among exactly those dimensions, by the time we reach `free`, all billed dims are known. So `input.dims[dimension].status === "known" &&` is always true — redundant condition. Minor maintainability. Hmm, but is it truly redundant? Yes — the for loop iterates the same TRANSCRIPTION_BILLED_DIMENSIONS array and returns on unknown. So the `status === "known"` conjunct is dead/redundant. Also TypeScript narrowing: after the loop, TS doesn't narrow `input.dims[dimension]` (indexed access), so the author added the check to satisfy TS — that's why `amount` access works. If they removed the status check, TS would complain about accessing `.amount` on the union. So it's TS-motivated. An alternative that's cleaner: since `TRANSCRIPTION_BILLED_DIMENSIONS` is typed and the loop guards, one could compute free inside the loop... e.g. track `let allZero = true` in the same loop. That avoids double iteration and the redundant conjunct:

```ts
let free = true;
for (const dimension of TRANSCRIPTION_BILLED_DIMENSIONS) {
  const state = input.dims[dimension];
  if (state.status === "unknown") {
    return { enabled: false, issue: { code: "price_unknown", dimension, reason: state.reason } };
