That's a genuine improvement — single pass, no redundancy, no `every` with always-true conjunct. This is a low-severity maintainability finding. Is it worth reporting? The focus area asks about it. The "0.00" part is disproven by canonicalPriceAmount; the redundant status check part is real. I'll report as low/medium maintainability with the fold-into-loop suggestion, noting the canonicalization makes "0" comparison safe (so no change needed there).

3. Focus #1: the trust boundary — `dataSize = size` even when actual bytes are truncated/padded. Let me think hard about whether this is a real billing-integrity defect in THIS code.

Threat model: server prices measuredSeconds = dataSize/byteRate from header. Provider decodes actual audio. Two attack directions:

(a) Under-billing: attacker crafts WAV where header `data` size declares tiny value (e.g. 1 byte) but appends 1 hour of PCM payload after. A conformant WAV decoder reads `data` bytes only → plays tiny audio. Provider gets tiny audio → tiny cost. Server prices tiny duration. Consistent. Under-billing requires the provider's decoder to consume BEYOND the declared data size. Most decoders (ffmpeg, ffprobe duration) respect the chunk size... actually many tolerant decoders (e.g., ffmpeg's WAV demuxer, and notably some libraries like Python `wave`, and OpenAI's service behavior unknown) will read to EOF if the data chunk size is wrong or if there's trailing audio. ffmpeg historically handles broken headers by reading to EOF (it warns "Invalid chunk size... defaulting to..." hmm, actually for data chunk with size larger than file, ffmpeg truncates; with smaller declared size, ffmpeg respects chunk boundaries but some tools parse trailing chunks). It's genuinely provider-dependent.

