Craft's comment claims allow_always behaves as allow_once, but the value is persisted verbatim into the shared table where other modules treat it as a durable grant:
- store.consume() line 239-246: decision === "allow_always" requires an approvalRules row to exist, else returns false.
- private-share.ts line 160-169: treats decision === "allow_always" (within ONCE_WINDOW_MS) as "allowed" — well, that also accepts allow_once, so not distinct there... it accepts both equally. Not the best evidence.
- Better: store.consume() — craft rows decided allow_always with no rule row are un-consumable; any generic consumer path would break. Actually craft never calls consume (search found none), so consume impact latent.
- Strongest evidence for real cross-module consequence: The generic `decide` writes the rule; craft doesn't → row says "always allowed" but no rule exists → future consumers (consume(), rule displays, audit consumers) see a grant that was never issued. Suggest rejecting "allow_always" at this endpoint (treat as allow_once) or documenting/normalizing.

Category: bug; severity: medium-high. I'll say high? It's a semantic integrity issue in shared data with at least one concrete broken consumer path (consume returns false). Given no craft call path to consume today, medium~high. I'll use high since it violates a cross-module invariant with concrete downstream reader.

