Issues:
1. `release()` resolves `held` permanently. After the first `release()` call, all subsequent (and currently-hanging) hold requests resolve immediately. So a test that releases once, then flips a route to `hold` again for a second probe attempt will NOT actually hold — the request returns `{released:true}` instantly, silently invalidating timeout-behavior assertions.
2. Also, `respondWith` only replaces the global `plan`; requests already awaiting `held` stay held — that's arguably by design ("Release every request a hold plan is keeping open").

Fix: make the gate re-entrant — e.g., a counter/generation-based gate:
```ts
let release = () => {};
const held = new Promise<void>((r) => (release = r));
```
Better suggestion: track pending holders and create a fresh promise per request:
```ts
const pending = new Set<() => void>();
const gate = () =>
  new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
    pending.add(resolve);
  });
...
if (planned.kind === "hold") { await gate(); ... }
...
release() { for (const r of [...pending]) r(); pending.clear(); }
```

